Normal shocks, conservation and entropy selection (@fd-normal-shock: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Compressible-Flow-NACA-1135.lingua#r_5V6PTWHXNJS515S6PMX7BN2ZHM", #source "../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA", #source "../sources/Analytical-Fluid-Mechanics-Fitzpatrick.lingua#r_075CGXNA8WHV26ZW53C7XTQ48V", #fluid-dynamics "Oblique-shocks-expansion-fans-and-flow-turning.lingua#r_5WCHS2S4QN94C8N89S54YH0478") { r_3MN97APMW4SJQJ4G5GZF4JSSFX
**Read:** [Equations, Tables, and Charts for Compressible Flow — NACA 1135](../sources/Compressible-Flow-NACA-1135.lingua#r_5V6PTWHXNJS515S6PMX7BN2ZHM), normal-shock relations and tables; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 4 jump conditions and entropy; [Fluid Mechanics — Richard Fitzpatrick](../sources/Analytical-Fluid-Mechanics-Fitzpatrick.lingua#r_075CGXNA8WHV26ZW53C7XTQ48V), normal shocks.

Across a steady planar adiabatic shock, mass flux, normal momentum flux and total enthalpy are continuous when the far upstream/downstream states are uniform and no external work is added. In the shock frame, use ρ_1U_1=ρ_2U_2, p_1+ρ_1U_1²=p_2+ρ_2U_2² and h_1+U_1²/2=h_2+U_2²/2. The narrow internal structure contains irreversible processes even when the far-field jump is modelled as a discontinuity.

For γ=1.4 and upstream Mach number 2, the perfect-gas relations give p_2/p_1=4.5, ρ_2/ρ_1=8/3, T_2/T_1=27/16 and M_2=1/√3. Verify these independently from the three conserved fluxes. Total enthalpy is unchanged, while entropy rises and stagnation pressure falls. Reversing the jump algebraically does not produce an admissible expansion shock in this equilibrium perfect-gas model.

Derive the general ratios, inspect the weak-shock limit and compare with NACA tables. Distinguish a stationary shock from a moving shock observed in laboratory coordinates. The finite shock thickness and nonequilibrium chemistry require further kinetic and thermochemical descriptions when those are the quantities of interest.

